%S A401145 1,0,1,1,1,1,3,1,3,2,4,2,6,2,5,3,15,1,26,1,10,6,44,3,27,6,24,7,113,4,
%T A401145 138,9,29,14,73,5,273,12,57,13,483,8,588,19,53,31,920,9,651,17,170,38,
%U A401145 1714,16,581,37,279,63,3146,10,3765,88,228,89,1371,12,6693
%N A401145 Number of partitions of n into distinct parts such that n plus the product of the parts is prime.
%C A401145 If d > 1 divides both n and the product of the parts, then d divides n + product, which exceeds d, so n + product is composite. Hence only partitions into distinct parts that are all relatively prime to n can be counted.
%C A401145 For even n, the case d = 2 shows that only partitions into distinct odd parts (necessarily an even number of them) can be counted. For odd n > 1 there is a separate parity restriction: if the product were odd, n + product would be even and greater than 2, so at least one even part is required (in addition to all parts being relatively prime to n).
%C A401145 For n = 1 the only partition is 1 itself, giving 1 + 1 = 2, which is prime, so a(1) = 1.
%C A401145 Consequently a(n) <= A057562(n), the number of partitions of n into parts (not necessarily distinct) that are all relatively prime to n.
%C A401145 Conjecture: for n >= 7, a(n) > max(a(n-1), a(n+1)).
%C A401145 Conjecture: a(n) > 0 for all n except for n = 2. This holds for n <= 170.
%H A401145 Sean A. Irvine, <a href="/A401145/b401145.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..170</a>
%e A401145 a(2) = 0: the only partition of 2 into distinct parts is 2 itself, and 2 + 2 = 4 is composite.
%e A401145 a(7) = 3: the partitions of 7 into distinct parts are 7, 6+1, 5+2, 4+3, 4+2+1, with 7 + product equal to 14, 13, 17, 19, 15 respectively. Exactly 13, 17 and 19 are prime.
%e A401145 a(10) = 2: the partitions of 10 into distinct parts, with 10 + product in parentheses, are 10 (20), 9+1 (19), 8+2 (26), 7+3 (31), 6+4 (34), 7+2+1 (24), 6+3+1 (28), 5+4+1 (30), 5+3+2 (40) and 4+3+2+1 (34). Only 19 and 31 are prime.
%t A401145 a[n_] := Module[{b},
%t A401145   b[m_, k_, p_] := If[m == 0, Boole[PrimeQ[p + n]],
%t A401145     Sum[b[m - j, j - 1, p*j], {j, 1, Min[m, k]}]];
%t A401145   b[n, n, 1]];
%t A401145 Table[a[n], {n, 1, 80}]
%o A401145 (Python)
%o A401145 from sympy import isprime
%o A401145 def a(n):
%o A401145     def rec(rem, m, pr):
%o A401145         if rem == 0:
%o A401145             return int(isprime(pr + n))
%o A401145         return sum(rec(rem - p, p - 1, pr * p) for p in range(min(rem, m), 0, -1))
%o A401145     return rec(n, n, 1)
%o A401145 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 81)])
%Y A401145 Cf. A000009, A057562.
%K A401145 nonn,new
%O A401145 1,7
